housands of everyday  activities as candidates and then did a  user study to ask how much would you as  a person benefit if a robot did this for  you this is intrinsically getting at the  question of what are the tasks that  humans will like robots help and we  learned something very interesting right  cleaning is by far one of the most  important tasks that humans want help  whether it's cleaning after a while  partying and cleaning a toilet and  cleaning walls and so on but there are  also tasks that humans don't want robots  to do such as plain squash or opening  Christmas gifts so we ranked all these  2000 tasks and took the top 1000 that  humans prefer more robotic or machine  help  and we use that to formulate the base of  our a thousand activities the the 1K  activities  now that we have established these  activities we need a user-friendly  language to describe what is the initial  state of each task and what you want the  robot to achieve and this is what we  call the bddl  language where it's a logical form  of of descriptions of the of what the  initial State and the goal state of each  of the tasks would look like and to  build this bddl database for each of  these tasks we actually use both human  annotation help as well as gpt3 and we  combine the strength of all these to put  together the the uh bddl annotation  so now that we have the tasks we have  the logical form to describe the tasks  we need an environment  we need to specify the kind of  environments and and features of this uh  of this uh data set or Benchmark and we  care a lot about the diversity of  objects so we already talked about we  have thousands of object models and some  of them have properties like cookable  sliceable freezable burnable deformable  and so on and many objects are  articulated with the moving opening and  and closing and and different kind of  articulations with joints and and axes  and so on we also have environments  ranging from houses hotels grocery  stores restaurants schools Gardens Halls  to offices  add  with all these prepare we need to build  an environment to simulate all these  tasks to allow embodied AI agencies  agents to perform these tasks in this  environment and this is uh  um this is what uh we are set out to do  simulation is really important they are  fast for training they are safe and they  you can  there can be easier ways to overcome  real-world biases and can be  reproducible for Benchmark and there has  been a lot of wonderful assimilation  environments built in robotic learning  we're just showing a few here from  Habitat to AI to Thor to 3D World to  Sapien and so on and they both come with  its own strength but what we really want  to focus on is not only the ability to  interact with the objects but some of  the key abilities like the changes of  temperature wetness deformability and so  on so this is just say I figure to show  that our our Behavior simulator has  covers some of the key abilities in in  in this simulated world and we are we  have officially  combined our effort with nvidia's  Omniverse so create an environment we  call Omni Gibson because it came from  our previous work I Gibson and Omni  Gibson is this uh realistic simulation  environment where we can simulate  thermal effects transition  transition machines lights and  Reflections fluids deform mobility and  transparency and  we actually did a user study on the  realism photorealism in the rendering of  these different assimilation environment  and here the perceptual the user score  the higher the score the more realistic  it is so the users give us a pretty high  score for only Gibson compared to other  simulated environments and I've already  shown you uh one video here's another  video of what Omni Gibson can render  with a lot of High Fidelity details high  resolution and and realism  so last but not the least we can start  to evaluate embody AI algorithms using  Behavior 1K data set there is a long way  to go there's no algorithm today that  can perform a thousand tasks and we  actually just selected very few and as  selected a couple of state of the art at  that time papers to to evaluate to get a  baseline understanding of how today's  algorithms do in Behavior Benchmark one  is the soft actor critic uh deep  reinforcement learning paper the other  one is the PPO paper and we first asked  to evaluate the skill levels such as  grasping or soaking towel or cleaning  stained shelf or cooking meat or slicing  fruits and the bottom line is that  um  um  if we  provide a simplified magic action at the  end our algorithm the the state of the  art algorithm can do relatively okay  with these short skill tasks which are  the blue light but as soon as you allow  you do not allow that simplified and  active action  um suddenly it drops to the orange line  and if you increase the object posts and  object appearance diversity the  algorithm performance also drops sharply  and and  of course  just start this  oh  ah let me try it and I'm probably just  gonna  um  of course uh  um  it's not only it's not enough  uh oh here is a example of uh RL  algorithm uh trying to decorate a store  and uh um  you know the this is uh uh this is where  we're we're trying to show uh the kind  of long Horizon tasks that behavior  aspires to do instead of just these  short uh short skill level uh tasks such  as grasping or slicing and again  um using a task uh like like the score  store decoration or collecting trash or  clean table  um we have to  give a lot of assumptions for the  algorithm to perform reasonably  assumptions of you know uh privileged  access to the action Primitives and the  state of the objects if we relax all  these assumptions the algorithm performs  very badly pretty much Zero Performance  and this is kind of exciting because it  shows these tasks are extremely complex  and there is a long way to go in  achieving these tasks and uh  last but not the least we are also  considering some to rea